tel 1.0

base murmuration
import soundness

info   the collection typeclasses, the operations built on them, and the one-shot `Iterator`
detail
    _Murmuration_ is the vocabulary of Soundness's collections: the typeclasses which say what
    a collection *can do* — be traversed, be rebuilt in its own shape, be mapped, be reversed,
    be converted to another shape, be tested for membership — and the operations written once
    over those typeclasses, so that `map`, `filter`, `bind`, `fold`, `group`, `sweep` and the
    rest read the same whether the receiver is a `List`, a `Set`, a `Map`, a `Chain`, a frozen
    `Array`, a `Text` (which traverses as its characters) or a Java collection. The collection
    *types* themselves — `List`, `Sequence`, `Set`, `Map`, `Ledger`, the lazy `Chain` and the
    frozen `Array[element]^{}` (with `Data` as `Array[Byte]^{}`) — are proscenium's opaque
    wrappers over the standard library's structures; murmuration supplies the instances which
    give each of them the vocabulary, and the vocabulary itself.

    The design has three layers. `Traversable` produces a fresh, one-shot `Iterator` over a
    value's elements, and is the only thing an operation needs in order to *read* a collection.
    `Reshapable` is the rebuild relation: from a source shape and a new element type it selects
    the natural result shape and builds it, so `filter` on a `Set` gives a `Set`, `remap` on a
    `Map` gives a `Map` when the results are pairs and a `List` when they are not, and `filter`
    on `Text` gives `Text`. Its sub-trait `Reshapable.Stable` marks the shapes which preserve
    order, and the order-sensitive operations (`sort`, `order`, `distinct`, `zip`, `excerpt`,
    `span`, `batched`, `trace`) demand it, so they are honestly *absent* from `Set` and `Map`
    rather than defined to do something arbitrary. `Mappable` is the third: `map` is driven by
    it rather than by `Traversable` so that a `Map` maps its *values* and keeps its keys, while
    `remap` is the pairwise form.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import sortingAlgorithms.timsort

    val numbers: List[Int] = List(3, 1, 2)
    val ages = Map(t"Ada" -> 36, t"Bob" -> 41)

    numbers.map(_*2)                      // List(6, 2, 4)
    numbers.filter(_ > 1)                 // List(3, 2)
    numbers.bind { n => List(n, n) }      // List(3, 3, 1, 1, 2, 2)
    numbers.fold(0)(_ + _)                // 6
    numbers.sort                          // List(1, 2, 3)
    numbers.group(_%2)                    // Map(1 -> List(3, 1), 0 -> List(2))
    ages.map(_ + 1)                       // Map(t"Ada" -> 37, t"Bob" -> 42): values mapped, keys kept
    ages.remap { (name, age) => name }    // List(t"Ada", t"Bob")
    t"hello".filter(_ != 'l')             // t"heo"
    ```

    A few rules of the vocabulary are worth knowing before the first surprise:

    - **Names.** `bind` is the monadic bind; `flatMap` is the same operation and exists only
      because `for`-comprehensions desugar to it. `each` (in rudiments) is the loop, `foreach`
      exists for the same desugaring reason. `fold` is the stdlib's `foldLeft`, `trace` its
      `scanLeft`, `sweep` its `collect`, `group` its `groupBy`, `batched` its `grouped`,
      `excerpt` its `slice`, `deduplicate` its `distinctBy`, `has` its `contains`, `flat` its
      `flatten`, `keep`/`skip` (rudiments) its `take`/`drop`, `order` its `sortBy`, and
      `except` its `diff`. There is no `filterNot`: it is written `filter(!predicate(_))`.
    - **Overloads by value and by predicate.** Where a method could take either a value to
      compare or a predicate to apply, both are offered under one name wherever the element
      type allows it. `count` and `where` are the exception: with a path-dependent element
      type a value overload is ambiguous with every lambda, so `count(_ == value)` is the
      idiom for counting one element.
    - **Cost is acknowledged, not hidden.** Operations whose cost is worse than their name
      suggests — `size` and `last` on a `List`, `size` on a `Chain`, positional `at` on a
      `List` — are gated behind a named given from denominative's `dysasymptotics` package
      (`import dysasymptotics.linearSize`, `linearAccess`, `unboundedSize`), imported *by
      name* in the file or block that accepts the cost. `nil` (emptiness) is O(1) everywhere
      and never gated. Do not dodge a gate with `.stdlib`: open it where the cost is right.
    - **Conversion** is `xs.to[List]`, `xs.to[Set]`, `pairs.to[Map]`, `chars.to[Text]`,
      `xs.to[Array]` (rudiments' `to`, over `Convertible`): the *bracket* form, naming the
      shape as a type. The parenthesised `xs.to(List)` of the standard library survives only
      on stdlib receivers, which have a member `to` of their own. (An earlier `transmute[T]`
      was this operation under a temporary name; it is `to[T]` now.)
    - **Sorting names its algorithm.** `sort` and `order` (rudiments) need a `SortAlgorithm`
      given in scope, chosen by importing one of `sortingAlgorithms` by name — there is no
      default, and two imports are an ambiguity — and a `Comparable` for the element or key.
    - **The standard library is a boundary.** `xs.stdlib` on any opaque collection exposes the
      underlying structure without copying, and `List.from(…)`, `Set.from(…)`, … come back.
      Every such crossing is greppable, which is what lets the rest of a program stay inside
      the total vocabulary. A `Traversable` instance also exists for `java.lang.Iterable`,
      `java.util.Iterator`, `java.util.Map` and `java.util.Enumeration`, so
      `javaList.to[List]` needs no conversion at all.
    - **Arrays are not collections.** The frozen `Array[e]^{}` is `Traversable`, `Reshapable`
      and `Convertible`, so it can be read, mapped and converted, but it is edited by copy
      (`define`, `omit`, `Array.concat`, `Array.thaw`/`Array.freeze`, in proscenium and
      denominative), not through a `List`-like surface; the mutable `Array[e]^` sorts in place
      with `sort()`.

    The library's one concrete type is `Iterator[element]`: a separation-checked, one-shot,
    non-allocating pull over a source, which replaces `scala.Iterator` throughout Soundness.
    `Traversable.traverse` produces one and every operation consumes one, so it is mostly
    internal currency, but it is also the honest type for anything read from the outside world
    exactly once — a socket's frames, a process's lines, a terminal's keypresses — and the
    Soundness name `Iterator` now means this type.

    `murmuration.core` is a single source set, compiled for the JVM, Scala.js and Scala
    Native. It sits *below* denominative and vacuous, so nothing here knows about `Ordinal` or
    `Optional`: the operations which return an `Optional` (`head`, `last`, `find`, `where`,
    `reap`) or take an `Ordinal` (`each`, `at`, `indexed`) are defined in rudiments over these
    same typeclasses. Everything is exported to the `soundness` umbrella, and rudiments
    re-exports it too, so `import soundness.*` (or `import rudiments.*`, never both — the
    re-exports would make every operation ambiguous) is all a user writes; the
    `sortingAlgorithms` choice package merges with the umbrella's own.

refer murmuration.Traversable
refer murmuration.Reshapable
refer murmuration.Mappable
refer murmuration.Iterator
refer murmuration.sortingAlgorithms
refer denominative.dysasymptotics

entry .Traversable   a type whose elements can be visited in order, as `Self is Traversable by Operand`
  detail
      `Traversable` is the one thing an operation needs in order to *read* a collection. An
      instance says what the elements are (its `Operand`, bound with `by`: `List[Int] is
      Traversable by Int`, `Map[k, v] is Traversable by (k, v)`, `Text is Traversable by
      Char`) and how to produce a fresh, one-shot `Iterator` over them (`#traverse`). Every
      operation in murmuration and rudiments which only reads — `exists`, `fold`, `count`,
      `has` via `Inclusive`, `head`, `find`, `each`, and the input side of `map`, `filter`,
      `bind`, `group` and `sort` — takes it as evidence, and a new shape gains the whole
      vocabulary by supplying one instance.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      case class Bag(items: List[Text])

      object Bag:
        given traversable: Bag is Traversable by Text = bag => Iterator.from(List.iterator(bag.items))

      Bag(List(t"a", t"b")).count(_ == t"a")   // 1
      Bag(List(t"a", t"b")).to[Set]            // Set(t"a", t"b")
      ```

      Instances exist for the opaque `List`, `Chain`, `Sequence`, `Set`, `Map` (as pairs) and
      `Ledger` (as pairs), for the frozen array `Array[e]^{refs}` and its `Sized` form, for
      any `scala.Iterable` (`.iterable`, at lower priority), and for the Java `Iterable`,
      `Iterator`, `Map` and `Enumeration`. All live in this companion, so they resolve with no
      import. The collection instances are *subtype-parametric* (`list <: List[element]`), so
      a receiver typed `List[e] & Populated` — a `List(a, b)` literal, an `occupied` result —
      matches them as well as a plain one.

      Because `Text` is `Traversable`, every generic operation applies to text, and a
      text-only extension of the same name would be ambiguous at the umbrella: this is why
      gossamer contributes *instances* (through `traversableTextual`, `reversibleTextual`)
      rather than competing extensions.

      The iterator `traverse` returns is exclusive (`Iterator[Operand]^`): a traversal
      borrows, and it is consumed by exactly one operation. An `Iterator` is therefore
      deliberately *not* itself `Traversable` — it cannot be traversed twice — and drains with
      its own member `to[List]`.
  refer murmuration.Reshapable
  refer murmuration.Iterator
  refer murmuration.Convertible
  entry #traverse   a fresh one-shot `Iterator` over the value's elements, in order
      The iterator is exclusive (`Iterator[Operand]^`) and consumed by the operation that asked
      for it; it is internal currency, which rudiments' `xs.iterator` exposes when a caller
      wants to pull by hand.
  entry .list   the opaque `List`, element by element; matches `List[e] & Populated` too
  entry .chain   the lazy `Chain`, pulling elements on demand as the iterator advances
  entry .sequence   the indexed `Sequence`, element by element
  entry .set   the opaque `Set`, in its own (unordered) iteration order
  entry .map   the opaque `Map`, traversed as its `(key, value)` pairs
      The pair is the `Operand`, so `map.fold`, `map.filter`, `map.remap` and `map.count` see
      entries; `map.map` sees values, through `Mappable`.
  entry .ledger   the insertion-ordered `Ledger`, as `(key, value)` pairs in insertion order
  entry .frozenArray   the frozen array, read through any read-only reference
      `(Array[element]^{refs}) is Traversable by element`, capture-polymorphic in `refs` so a
      statically frozen `Array[e]^{}`, a boxed capture variable and an `any.rd` reference all
      match. Reading through a shared reference is sound under separation checking, which
      excludes a live writer wherever a reader aliases it.
  entry .sizedFrozenArray   a `Sized[length]` frozen array, by its own instance
      `(Array[element]^{refs} & Sized[length]) is Traversable by element`. A separate instance
      rather than a widening of `.frozenArray`, because a type variable bounded by a capturing
      type is elaborated as `array^{any.rd}` and would then fail evidence declared for the
      pure array; spelling the brand in the `Self` keeps the shape exact.
  entry .javaIterable   any `java.lang.Iterable` — `java.util.List`, `Set`, `Collection`, …
      Subtype-parametric, so a `java.util.ArrayList[Int]` or a `jar.Attributes` resolves.
      `javaList.to[List]` and `javaList.each(…)` need no `asScala`.
  entry .javaIterator   a `java.util.Iterator`, taken over for its remaining elements
  entry .javaMap   a `java.util.Map`, traversed as `(key, value)` pairs
      Invariant in both parameters (unlike `.javaIterable`): a two-parameter subtype-parametric
      instance let the compiler speculatively treat unrelated opaque types as Java maps. A
      subtype such as `jar.Attributes` is ascribed to `java.util.Map[Object, Object]` first.
  entry .javaEnumeration   a `java.util.Enumeration`, taken over for its remaining elements

entry .Traversable2   the parent trait holding the lower-priority `Iterable` instance
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: `object Traversable` extends it so that the blanket `scala.Iterable`
      instance ranks below the per-alias instances in the companion, which the compiler would
      otherwise report as ambiguous for an alias receiver.
  entry #iterable   any `scala.Iterable[element]` is `Traversable by element`
      The fallback for raw standard-library collections (`Vector`, `Range`, `IndexedSeq`, …),
      through their own `iterator`. Lower priority than every instance in `Traversable`'s
      companion.

entry .Reshapable   how a source shape rebuilds itself from new elements, as `Self is Reshapable by Operand to Result`
  detail
      `Reshapable` is the rebuild relation behind every transforming operation: `remap`,
      `bind`, `filter`, `sweep`, `group`, `partition`, `flat` and the rest read their source
      through `Traversable` and hand the resulting iterator to `#reshape`, which builds the
      result. The instance is selected per *(source shape, new element type)*, which lets
      results be more precise than a class hierarchy allows: a `List` of anything is a `List`;
      a `Set` of anything a `Set`; a `Map` rebuilt from pairs is a `Map` (`.map`), from
      anything else a `List` (`.mapToList`), and — when an order-sensitive operation asks for a
      `Stable` result — a `Ledger` (`.mapToLedger`); `Text` rebuilds from its own characters,
      and from nothing else.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Map(1 -> t"a").remap { (key, value) => (key + 1, value) }   // Map(2 -> t"a")
      Map(1 -> t"a").remap { (key, value) => key }                // List(1)
      Set(1, 2, 3, 4).sweep { case n if n%2 == 0 => n*10 }       // Set(20, 40)
      t"hello".filter(_ != 'l')                                   // t"heo"
      ```

      `.Stable` marks the instances whose shape preserves order. Operations whose meaning
      depends on order — `sort` and `order`, `distinct`, `deduplicate`, `zip`, `excerpt`,
      `span`, `trace`, `batched`, rudiments' `indexed` — demand `Reshapable.Stable`, so they do
      not exist on `Set` or `Map`: a sorted set would drop its order again on the way out, and
      the compiler says so instead. `partition` and `sweep` are deliberately *not* `Stable`,
      since gathering from an unordered shape is meaningful.

      `reshape` takes a *capturing* iterator (`Iterator[Operand]^`), because the iterators the
      operations produce capture the caller's lambda. Every instance but `.chain` drains it
      eagerly, so nothing escapes; the chain is the deliberate exception and keeps the source's
      laziness. A strict instance of your own is a one-liner through `.build`:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      case class Bag(items: List[Text])

      object Bag:
        given reshapable: Bag is Reshapable by Text to Bag =
          elements => Bag(List.from(Reshapable.build(elements, scala.collection.immutable.List.newBuilder[Text])))
      ```
  refer murmuration.Traversable
  refer murmuration.Mappable
  entry #reshape   builds the `Result` from an iterator of new elements
      The iterator is exclusive and may capture the lambda that produced its elements; a strict
      instance consumes it completely inside the call.
  entry .Stable   the sub-trait of instances whose shape preserves element order
      Extended by the `List`, `Sequence`, `IndexedSeq`, `Chain`, frozen-array, `Ledger` and
      (to a `Ledger` or a `List`) `Map` instances, and not by `.set` or `.map`. An operation
      which demands `self is Reshapable.Stable by e to r` is thereby unavailable on an
      unordered receiver, which is how `Set(3, 1, 2).sort` fails to compile rather than
      returning a set in no particular order. The name is about a *shape* keeping order, not
      about a sorting algorithm being stable; see `SortAlgorithm` for the latter.
  entry .list   a `List` rebuilds as a `List` of the new element type (stable)
      Subtype-parametric in the source (`list <: List[element]`), so a `List[e] & Populated`
      receiver reshapes too; the result is a plain `List[element2]`, since a transformation may
      empty it.
  entry .set   a `Set` rebuilds as a `Set` (unordered, not stable)
  entry .sequence   a `Sequence` rebuilds as a `Sequence` (stable)
  entry .indexedSeq   a stdlib `IndexedSeq` rebuilds as an `IndexedSeq` (stable)
  entry .chain   a `Chain` rebuilds lazily as a `Chain`, pulling from the iterator as it is forced
      The one non-strict instance: the iterator escapes into the chain, which memoizes what it
      has pulled. `naturals.filter(_%2 == 0)` on an infinite chain is therefore fine.
  entry .frozenArray   a frozen `Array[e]^{}` rebuilds as a fresh frozen `Array[e2]^{}`
      Needs a `ClassTag` for the new element type. The rebuilt array is fresh, so freezing it is
      discharged by construction.
  entry .map   a `Map` rebuilt from `(key2, value2)` pairs is a `Map[key2, value2]`
      The highest-priority `Map` instance: `remap` and pairwise `bind` with pair results stay a
      `Map`. Not `Stable`; a stable pair-rebuild falls through to `.Fallback`'s `mapToLedger`.
  entry .ledger   a `Ledger` rebuilt from pairs is a `Ledger`, and legitimately stable
      Insertion order is a `Ledger`'s identity, so it carries the whole order-sensitive surface:
      `ledger.order(…)`, `distinct`, `trace`, `zip`.
  entry .Fallback   the middle rung of the `Map`/`Ledger` priority ladder
    detail
        A parent trait of `Reshapable`'s companion, so its instances rank below those declared
        in the companion itself and above `.Fallback2`'s. The ladder decides what a `Map` or
        `Ledger` reshapes into when the lambda's result is not a pair, or when a stable result
        is demanded, without ambiguity.
    entry #mapToLedger   a stable pair-rebuild of a `Map` yields a `Ledger`
        `Map[k, v] is Reshapable.Stable by (k2, v2) to Ledger[k2, v2]`: an unordered `Map`
        cannot honestly receive an order-sensitive result, but the insertion-ordered `Ledger`
        can, so `ages.order(_(1))` is a `Ledger` iterating in sorted order. Lower priority than
        `.map`, so a plain `filter` or `remap` still rebuilds the cheaper `Map`.
    entry #ledgerToList   a `Ledger` rebuilt from non-pair elements is a `List`, order preserved
  entry .Fallback2   the lowest rung: a `Map` rebuilt from non-pairs is a `List`
    detail
        The parent of `.Fallback`, holding the instance that must lose to every other `Map`
        instance.
    entry #mapToList   a `Map` rebuilt from non-pair elements is a `List` (stable)
        `ages.remap { (name, age) => name }` is a `List[Text]` — a more precise result than the
        standard library's `Iterable`.
  entry .build   drains an iterator into a stdlib `Builder` and returns what it built
      The strict rebuild, for writing an instance of your own over a standard-library
      builder: `Reshapable.build(elements, sci.Vector.newBuilder[e])`. Public only because the
      instances in the companion are lambdas; it is the one place the layer meets a builder.

entry .Mappable   the shape-preserving `map`: a container, what its lambda sees, and the mapped container
  detail
      `map` is driven by `Mappable` rather than by `Traversable` plus `Reshapable`, for one
      reason: a `Map` should map its *values* and keep its keys, where a traversal would hand
      the lambda `(key, value)` pairs. An instance fixes, from the container alone, what the
      lambda receives (`Operand`: the element, or the value for a `Map`) and the mapped
      container's constructor (`Result[_]`, of kind `* -> *`, which is why this is not a
      `Resultant` typeclass). Both are decided before the lambda is elaborated, so
      `xs.map(_.field)` infers the element type.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      List(1, 2, 3).map(_*2)                   // List(2, 4, 6)
      Set(1, 2, 3).map(_*2)                    // Set(2, 4, 6)
      Map(t"a" -> 1, t"b" -> 2).map(_*10)      // Map(t"a" -> 10, t"b" -> 20)
      Map(t"a" -> 1).remap { (k, v) => (k, v + 1) }   // Map(t"a" -> 2): the pairwise form
      ```

      The instances are subtype-parametric (`container <: List[element]`), which is what
      matches a `List[e] & Populated` receiver and the umbrella's re-export aliases; a raw
      stdlib collection falls back to `.Fallback`'s `iterable` and maps to a `List`. One trap
      follows from that: a stdlib `scala.collection.immutable.Map` reached through the
      fallback maps its *entries*, not its values, so a file which means Soundness's `Map`
      should make sure the name resolves to `proscenium.Map` (it does under `import
      soundness.*` unless a stdlib `Map` import shadows it).

      The extension `map` is a plain `def` rather than `inline`: an inline extension re-infers
      the result per call site under capture checking and leaks fresh capabilities.
  refer murmuration.map
  refer murmuration.remap
  refer murmuration.Reshapable
  entry #Result   the mapped container's constructor, applied to the new element type
  entry #map   maps every `Operand` through the lambda into `Result[element2]`
  entry .list   a `List` maps to a `List` of the lambda's results
  entry .set   a `Set` maps to a `Set` (through the `Set.map` primitive, whose result is pure)
  entry .sequence   a `Sequence` maps to a `Sequence`
  entry .chain   a `Chain` maps lazily to a `Chain`
  entry .map   a `Map` maps its values, keeping its keys: `Operand` is the value, `Result[v2] = Map[key, v2]`
  entry .ledger   a `Ledger` maps its values in insertion order, keeping its keys
  entry .Fallback   the lower-priority instance for raw standard-library collections
    detail
        A parent trait of `Mappable`'s companion, so its one instance loses to every alias
        instance declared in the companion.
    entry #iterable   any `scala.Iterable[element]` maps to a `List[element2]`
        Keeps `.map` working on ranges, vectors and other raw collections, as the old generic
        `map` did. A stdlib `Map` reached this way has `(key, value)` as its operand.

entry .Convertible   conversion to a requested shape, `Self is Convertible in Form to Result`
  detail
      `Convertible` is what rudiments' `xs.to[Form]` resolves: `Form` names the target — an
      unapplied constructor such as `List`, `Set`, `Map`, `Sequence`, `Chain`, `Array`,
      `java.util.List`, or a proper type such as `Text` — and `Result` is the fully-applied
      result implicit search derives for it. `Form` is declared `<: AnyKind`, so one `to`
      serves both kinds, unlike the standard library's `Factory`-taking `to(List)`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val pairs = List(t"a" -> 1, t"b" -> 2)
      pairs.to[Map]                  // Map(t"a" -> 1, t"b" -> 2)
      Set(3, 1, 2).to[List]          // a List, in the set's iteration order
      t"abc".to[List]                // List('a', 'b', 'c')
      List(1, 2, 3).to[Array]        // Array(1, 2, 3): frozen, Array[Int]^{}
      List(1, 2).to[java.util.List]  // a fresh java.util.ArrayList
      ```

      Every instance here derives from `Traversable`, so anything traversable — a Soundness
      collection, a `Text`, a Java collection, a value with its own `Traversable` instance —
      converts to any of these shapes with no further instance. Conversion *to* a type such
      as `Text` is supplied by that type (gossamer), in its own companion.

      Spelling: `xs.to[List]` (bracket) is the normal form. A parenthesised `xs.to(List)`
      survives only where a stdlib receiver's own member `to` wins; on a `Set`, `Array` or
      `Data` receiver the parenthesised form can even be ambiguous, and the bracket form is the
      fix. An `Iterator` is not `Traversable` and so not `Convertible`: it drains with its own
      member `iterator.to[List]`. The `to` extension is `transparent inline`, so where nothing
      pins the expected type (`xs.to[List] == List(1, 2)` in an assertion) the comparison may
      need the receiver bound to a typed `val` first.
  refer murmuration.Traversable
  entry #Form   the requested shape: a type constructor (`List`, `Map`) or a proper type (`Text`)
  entry #convert   builds the `Result` from the value
  entry .list   any `Traversable` to a `List` of its elements
  entry .set   any `Traversable` to a `Set` of its elements
  entry .sequence   any `Traversable` to a `Sequence`
  entry .indexedSeq   any `Traversable` to a stdlib `IndexedSeq`
  entry .chain   any `Traversable` to a `Chain`, pulled lazily from the traversal
  entry .map   any `Traversable` of `(key, value)` pairs to a `Map`
  entry .ledger   any `Traversable` of `(key, value)` pairs to an insertion-ordered `Ledger`
  entry .javaList   any `Traversable` to a fresh, mutable `java.util.ArrayList`
      The Java boundary: `xs.to[java.util.List]` builds the platform collection directly from
      the traversal, with no stdlib bridge at the call site, and the receiving API owns the
      result outright.
  entry .frozenArray   any `Traversable` to a frozen `Array[element]^{}`
      Needs a `ClassTag` for the element. The built array is fresh, so no writer survives its
      construction and the result is pure.

entry .Inclusive   a type whose values can be tested for membership of a value, as `Self is Inclusive by Operand`
  detail
      Backs `collection.has(value)`. The queried type is the instance's `Operand`, fixed to the
      collection's *exact* element type, so `list.has(wrongType)` is a compile error, and so is
      `map.has(key)`: a `Map`'s element is a `(key, value)` pair, and whether a *key* is present
      is `Applicable`'s `defines` (rudiments), not membership. Instances exist for `List` (a
      single linear pass, so ungated), `Set`, `Sequence`, the frozen array, the bare JVM array,
      any `scala.Iterable` (`.Fallback`), and — in gossamer — `Text` by `Char`; substring
      containment is `subsumes` instead.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      List(1, 2, 3).has(2)       // true
      Set(t"a", t"b").has(t"c")  // false
      t"hello".has('e')          // true
      ```
  refer murmuration.has
  entry #has   whether `value` occurs among the elements
  entry .Element   the exact element type of a `scala.Iterable`, as a match type
      `Element[collection]` reduces `Iterable[element]` to `element`, which is how `.Fallback`'s
      instance fixes the queried type exactly rather than widening it to `Matchable` through
      covariance.
  entry .Fallback   the lower-priority parent holding the `scala.Iterable` instance
    detail
        A parent of `Inclusive`'s companion, so an unrefined `X is Inclusive` query on an
        opaque collection is not reported ambiguous against the per-alias instances.
    entry #iterable   any `scala.Iterable` is `Inclusive by` its exact element, through `exists`
  entry .frozenArray   a frozen `Array[element]^{}` is `Inclusive by element`, by a linear scan
  entry .sequence   a `Sequence` is `Inclusive by` its element
  entry .set   a `Set` is `Inclusive by` its element, by hashing
  entry .list   a `List` is `Inclusive by` its element, by one linear pass (ungated)
  entry .array   a bare `scala.Array[element]` is `Inclusive by element`

entry .Intersectable   set algebra over an abstract receiver: `intersect` and `except`
  detail
      `left.intersect(right)` keeps the elements present in both and `left.except(right)`
      removes `right`'s elements (the standard library's `diff`). Union is not here: for an
      unordered unique shape it is plain concatenation, which symbolism's `Concatenable`
      already provides as `left + right`, and a single element is added with `:+`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val primes = Set(2, 3, 5, 7)
      primes.intersect(Set(2, 4))   // Set(2)
      primes.except(Set(2, 3))      // Set(5, 7)
      primes :+ 11                  // Set(2, 3, 5, 7, 11)
      ```

      The one instance, `.set`, is deliberately not subtype-parametric: an intersection can
      empty a non-empty receiver, so a `& Populated` brand must not survive it.
  refer murmuration.intersect
  refer murmuration.except
  entry #intersect   the elements present in both `left` and `right`
  entry #except   `left` without the elements of `right`
  entry .set   `Set[element] is Intersectable` with another `Set[element]` as its `Operand`

entry .intersect   `left.intersect(right)`: the elements present in both, for any `Intersectable`
    An extension over `self is Intersectable by operand`; on a `Set`, `right` is another `Set`
    of the same element type and the result is a `Set`.

entry .except   `left.except(right)`: `left` without `right`'s elements, for any `Intersectable`
    The standard library's `diff`, under a name which reads as a sentence. On a `Set`, `right`
    is another `Set` of the same element type.

entry .Keyed   the keyed-container surface: `keys` and `values`, typed by the container
  detail
      Better typed than the standard library's `Iterable` results: a `Map`'s keys are a `Set`
      (unique and unordered), a `Ledger`'s are a `List` (insertion order is its identity), and
      both containers' values are a `List`. The result types are declared as refinements on the
      instances (`type Keys = Set[key]`), so the extensions can bind them as type parameters
      and the umbrella's export forwarder reproduces them.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val ages = Map(t"Ada" -> 36, t"Bob" -> 41)
      ages.keys     // Set(t"Ada", t"Bob")
      ages.values   // List(36, 41)
      ```
  refer murmuration.keys
  refer murmuration.values
  entry #Keys   the type `keys` returns: `Set[key]` for a `Map`, `List[key]` for a `Ledger`
  entry #Values   the type `values` returns: `List[value]` for both
  entry #keys   the container's keys, as `Keys`
  entry #values   the container's values, as `Values`
  entry .map   `Map[key, value] is Keyed` with `Keys = Set[key]` and `Values = List[value]`
  entry .ledger   `Ledger[key, value] is Keyed` with `Keys = List[key]` and `Values = List[value]`, both in insertion order

entry .keys   `container.keys`: the keys of any `Keyed` container, as its `Keys` type
    A `Set[key]` for a `Map`, a `List[key]` in insertion order for a `Ledger`.

entry .values   `container.values`: the values of any `Keyed` container, as a `List`

entry .Reversible   order reversal, `Self is Reversible to Result`, unified over collections and text
  detail
      One `reverse` extension serves `List`, `Sequence` and every textual type, because
      gossamer contributes a `Reversible` *instance* (`reversibleTextual`, published in each
      textual type's companion) rather than a competing text-only extension, which would have
      clashed with this one at the umbrella. `Set` and `Map` have no instance: reversing an
      unordered shape means nothing, so `set.reverse` does not compile.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      List(3, 1, 2).reverse   // List(2, 1, 3)
      t"stressed".reverse     // t"desserts"
      ```

      The instances are subtype-parametric, so a `List[e] & Populated` reverses too, and the
      `Result` is bound as an ordinary type parameter at the extension so that the umbrella
      forwarder can reproduce it.
  refer murmuration.reverse
  entry #reverse   the value with its elements in the opposite order
  entry .list   a `List` reverses to a `List`
  entry .sequence   a `Sequence` reverses to a `Sequence`

entry .reverse   `value.reverse`: the elements in reverse order, for any `Reversible` value
    Lists and sequences reverse to their own shape; a `Text` (or any textual type, through
    gossamer's instance) reverses as its characters. Absent on `Set` and `Map`.

entry .SortAlgorithm   a sorting algorithm: puts a scratch array in order against a comparator
  detail
      An algorithm is nothing but a way of ordering an array of elements against a
      `java.util.Comparator`, and that is all the trait is. Every operation which sorts —
      rudiments' `sort` and `order` over a collection, and `sort()` over a mutable array —
      is that plus the arrangements for getting elements into an array and back out, so the
      algorithm is not parameterised by the collection it serves. (It once was; every
      implementation drained its receiver into an array anyway, and the one that did not was
      five to seven times slower.)

      Which algorithm sorts is the `SortAlgorithm` given in scope, chosen by importing one of
      `sortingAlgorithms` by name. There is deliberately no default: with none in scope a
      `sort` does not compile, and with two it is ambiguous. The six are:

      ```tabular
      column  Algorithm
      column  Stable
      column  In place
      column  Adaptive
      column  Notes
      row  `timsort`  yes  no  yes  the JDK's own `Arrays.sort` for objects, behind the standard library's `sorted`
      row  `powersort`  yes  no  yes  Timsort's merge policy replaced by a nearly-optimal binary merge tree (Munro and Wild, 2018), with galloping merges; the one for partly-ordered data
      row  `quicksort`  no  yes  no  median-of-three pivots with Hoare partitioning, insertion sort below twelve elements, recursion bounded at log₂(n) frames
      row  `heapsort`  no  yes  no  no quadratic worst case
      row  `insertionSort`  yes  yes  yes  only worth it for tiny input
      row  `bubbleSort`  yes  yes  yes  only worth it for tiny input; stops after a pass with no swap
      ```

      Stability — whether equal elements keep their source order — is a property of the
      algorithm, not of the types: `pairs.order(_(0))` under `timsort` leaves tied pairs in
      their original order, under `quicksort` it may not. It is not to be confused with
      `Reshapable.Stable`, which is about a *shape* preserving order.

      The algorithms are ordinary methods, deliberately not `inline`: one shared, compact loop
      is inlined by the JIT better than a copy per call site, which was measured to be slower
      (6.68 ms against 5.59 ms for a hundred thousand elements). A custom algorithm is any
      value of this type, bound as a local given.
  refer murmuration.sortingAlgorithms
  entry #sort   orders `array` in place, as `comparator` says

entry .sortingAlgorithms   the six sorting algorithms, each a given to import by name
  detail
      Every sort in Soundness — `sort`, `order` and the in-place `sort()` — takes a
      `SortAlgorithm`, and the choice is never implicit: `import soundness.*` brings none of
      these in, and exactly one is imported by name in the file or block that sorts. Two in
      scope make every sort ambiguous, which is the intended way of saying that a program sorts
      with one algorithm unless it says otherwise at the call site.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label No algorithm chosen
        code
            locally:
              List(3, 1, 2).sort   // does not compile
      frame
        label Timsort, the JDK's own
        code
            locally:
              import sortingAlgorithms.timsort
              List(3, 1, 2).sort   // List(1, 2, 3)
      frame
        label Powersort, for a stable sort of partly-ordered data
        code
            locally:
              import sortingAlgorithms.powersort
              List((1, t"a"), (0, t"b"), (1, t"c")).order(_(0))   // List((0, t"b"), (1, t"a"), (1, t"c")): ties keep their order
      frame
        label Two imports are an ambiguity
        code
            locally:
              import sortingAlgorithms.{timsort, quicksort}
              List(3, 1, 2).sort   // does not compile
      ##
      ```

      The block-scoped import is the idiom when one part of a program needs a different
      algorithm from the rest. The sort itself also needs a `Comparable` for the element (or
      for the key, under `order`): numbers and characters have one, a `Text` needs a collation
      chosen, and a type whose order is still a stdlib `Ordering` sorts through the bridge in
      `Comparable`'s companion.
  refer murmuration.SortAlgorithm
  entry .timsort   the JDK's `Arrays.sort` for objects: adaptive, stable, the stdlib's `sorted`
  entry .powersort   Powersort: Timsort's runs merged in near-optimal order, with galloping; stable
      Munro and Wild's 2018 algorithm. Input already in order is one run and is sorted by the
      scan which discovered that, with no merge buffer allocated at all.
  entry .quicksort   median-of-three quicksort, in place; not stable
      Hoare partitioning, insertion sort below twelve elements, recursion into the smaller
      side so the depth is bounded at log₂(n).
  entry .heapsort   heapsort, in place, with no quadratic worst case; not stable
  entry .bubbleSort   bubble sort, stable; a single pass on already-sorted input
  entry .insertionSort   insertion sort, stable; the right choice only for tiny input

entry .has   `collection.has(value)`: whether a value is among the elements
    An extension over any `Inclusive` receiver. The queried type is the exact element type,
    so a `Map` cannot be asked `has(key)` — a key is not an element; that is `defines`
    (rudiments). `List(1, 2, 3).has(2)`, `Set(t"a").has(t"b")`, `t"hello".has('e')`.

entry .map   `xs.map(lambda)`: the shape-preserving map, which on a `Map` maps the values
    Driven by `Mappable`, summoned at the extension so the lambda's parameter is the concrete
    element type before the lambda elaborates (`xs.map(_.field)` infers). A `List` maps to a
    `List`, a `Set` to a `Set`, a `Chain` lazily to a `Chain`, a `Map` to a `Map` with the
    same keys and mapped values, a `Ledger` likewise in order, and any raw `scala.Iterable` to
    a `List`. For the entries of a `Map` as pairs, use `remap`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    List(1, 2, 3).map(_*2)                 // List(2, 4, 6)
    Map(t"a" -> 1, t"b" -> 2).map(_*10)    // Map(t"a" -> 10, t"b" -> 20)
    ```

entry .remap   `xs.remap(lambda)`: the pairwise map, rebuilt through `Reshapable`
    The lambda receives each traversed element — a `Map`'s `(key, value)` pair — and the result
    takes whatever shape `Reshapable` derives for the source and the new element: a `Map`
    whose results are pairs stays a `Map`, one whose results are not becomes a `List`, and on
    a `List` `remap` is simply `map`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    Map(t"a" -> 1).remap { (key, value) => (key, value + 1) }   // Map(t"a" -> 2)
    Map(t"a" -> 1).remap { (key, value) => key }                // List(t"a")
    ```

entry .subsumes   `xs.subsumes(ys)`: whether `ys` occurs as a contiguous run within `xs`
    A substring test for `Text` (where gossamer's overload short-cuts through `indexOf`), and
    the same question for any `Traversable`: `List(1, 2, 3, 4).subsumes(List(2, 3))` is true,
    `List(1, 2, 3, 4).subsumes(List(2, 4))` false, and the empty subsequence is always
    present. Both sides must have the same type; a literal receiver typed `& Populated` cannot
    be asked about `List.empty` without first being bound to a plain `List`.

entry .bind   `xs.bind(lambda)`: the monadic bind, flattening a collection-valued lambda
    The preferred name for what the standard library calls `flatMap`. The lambda may return any
    `Traversable` — a different shape is fine, `List(1, 2).bind(n => Set(n))` is a `List` — and
    the result is rebuilt in the receiver's shape. `flatMap` is the same operation under the
    name `for`-comprehensions desugar to.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    List(1, 2).bind { n => List(n, n*10) }   // List(1, 10, 2, 20)
    ```

entry .flatMap   the same operation as `bind`, under the name `for`-comprehensions require
    Retained solely so that `for a <- xs; b <- f(a) yield …` desugars; at an explicit call site
    write `bind`.

entry .filter   `xs.filter(predicate)`: the elements satisfying the predicate, in the source's shape
    There is no `filterNot`: write `filter(!predicate(_))`. A `Set` filters to a `Set`, a
    `Chain` lazily to a `Chain`, a `Map` (by its pairs) to a `Map`, and `Text` to `Text`.

entry .withFilter   the `filter` that `for`-comprehension guards desugar to; strict
    Deliberately strict, unlike the standard library's lazy `WithFilter` carrier: a captured
    predicate would otherwise have to thread through every desugared stage under capture
    checking. `for n <- xs if n > 1 yield n` works as expected; nothing else should call it.

entry .foreach   `xs.foreach(lambda)`: visits every element; exists for `for` loops
    The name a `for (x <- xs) do …` loop desugars to. At an explicit call site the loop is
    rudiments' `each`, which also offers the element's ordinal as a contextual value.

entry .exists   `xs.exists(predicate)`: whether some element satisfies the predicate, stopping at the first

entry .fold   `xs.fold(initial)(lambda)`: accumulates a state over the elements, left to right
    The standard library's `foldLeft`, with the state's type fixed by `initial` (the stdlib
    `fold`'s widening to a supertype is not offered). `List(1, 2, 3).fold(0)(_ + _)` is `6`;
    on an empty receiver it is `initial`. A `Map` folds over its `(key, value)` pairs, so the
    lambda is written `{ case (acc, (k, v)) => … }` or with a two-parameter pattern.

entry .count   `xs.count(predicate)`: how many elements satisfy the predicate
    Only the predicate form: a value-taking overload would be ambiguous with it for every
    lambda argument, because the element type is path-dependent, so counting one element is
    `xs.count(_ == value)`.

entry .flat   `xss.flat`: one level of nesting removed, in the outer source's shape
    The inner values may be any `Traversable`, so a `List[Set[e]]` flattens to a `List[e]`
    and `List(List(1, 2), List(3)).flat` is `List(1, 2, 3)`. The standard library's
    `flatten`.

entry .trace   `xs.trace(initial)(lambda)`: every intermediate state of a fold, initial state first
    The standard library's `scanLeft`, in the source's own shape: `List(1, 2, 3).trace(0)(_ +
    _)` is `List(0, 1, 3, 6)`. Order-sensitive, so it demands `Reshapable.Stable` and is absent
    on `Set` and `Map`.

entry .excerpt   `xs.excerpt(from, until)`: the run of elements by position, end-exclusive and total
    The standard library's `slice`, by `Int` positions: `List(1, 2, 3, 4, 5).excerpt(1, 3)` is
    `List(2, 3)`. Bounds outside the source clamp rather than fail — `List(1, 2).excerpt(5,
    9)` is empty, `List(1, 2, 3).excerpt(2, 99)` is `List(3)`. Demands `Reshapable.Stable`.
    Positional segments by ordinal (`before`, `upto`, `from`, `after`, `keep`, `skip`) are
    rudiments' `Segmentable` vocabulary.

entry .zip   `xs.zip(ys)`: the elements paired positionally, stopping at the shorter side
    `ys` may be any `Traversable`; the pairs are rebuilt in `xs`'s shape, which must be
    `Reshapable.Stable`. `List(1, 2, 3).zip(List(t"a"))` is `List((1, t"a"))`. Pairing with
    positions is rudiments' `indexed`.

entry .group   `xs.group(lambda)`: the elements keyed by a function, into a `Map` of groups in the source's shape
    The standard library's `groupBy`: `List(1, 2, 3, 4).group(_%2)` is `Map(1 -> List(1, 3),
    0 -> List(2, 4))`. Each group is rebuilt in the receiver's own shape, so grouping a `Set`
    gives a `Map` of `Set`s.

entry .distinct   `xs.distinct`: repeated elements dropped, keeping the first occurrence of each
    Which occurrence survives is positional, so this demands `Reshapable.Stable` and is
    honestly unavailable on a `Set` (which has no repeats) or a `Map`.

entry .deduplicate   `xs.deduplicate(key)`: the first element for each value of `key`, in first-occurrence order
    The standard library's `distinctBy`: `List(10, 43, 22, 71, 52).deduplicate(_%10)` is
    `List(10, 43, 22, 71)`. Like `distinct`, positional and so `Stable`-gated.

entry .span   `xs.span(predicate)`: the longest leading run satisfying the predicate, and the remainder
    `List(1, 2, 3, 1).span(_ < 3)` is `(List(1, 2), List(3, 1))`. The two sides are traversed
    independently, since one iterator cannot be consumed twice. Order-sensitive, so
    `Stable`-gated. For a split which ignores position, see `partition`.

entry .partition   `xs.partition(predicate)`: the elements satisfying the predicate, and those not
    Unlike `span`, membership ignores position, so a `Set` or `Map` partitions meaningfully:
    `Set(1, 2, 3, 4).partition(_%2 == 0)` is `(Set(2, 4), Set(1, 3))`; on a `List` both sides
    keep their order.

entry .sweep   `xs.sweep { case … }`: filter and map in one pass, through a partial function
    The standard library's `collect`: `List(1, 2, 3, 4).sweep { case n if n%2 == 0 => n*10 }`
    is `List(20, 40)`. Not `Stable`-gated — gathering from a `Set` is meaningful. The partial
    function is capability-annotated, so a case body which raises through a `Tactic` is
    accepted; it is applied eagerly and retains nothing. Its single-result sibling is
    rudiments' `reap`.

entry .batched   `xs.batched(size)`: consecutive batches of at most `size` elements, as a `List` of the source's shape
    The standard library's `grouped`: `List(1, 2, 3, 4, 5).batched(2)` is `List(List(1, 2),
    List(3, 4), List(5))`, and the last batch may be shorter. Batch boundaries depend on
    order, so it is `Stable`-gated. Named `batched` rather than `chunked`, which is turbulence's
    byte-stream operation.

entry .Iterator   a one-shot, separation-checked pull over a source of elements; Soundness's `Iterator`
  detail
      `Iterator[element]` is the collection layer's internal currency — `Traversable.traverse`
      produces one and every operation consumes one — and the honest type of anything read from
      the outside world exactly once: a socket's frames, a process's output lines, a terminal's
      keypresses, an archive's entries as each payload opens. It replaces `scala.Iterator`
      throughout Soundness: under `import soundness.*` (or `import rudiments.*`) the bare name
      `Iterator` is this type, and a stdlib iterator is spelt `scala.collection.Iterator`.

      Unlike the standard library's, it is capture- and separation-checked, and it has no way
      to ask for an element that is not there. It extends `caps.Stateful`, so a value typed
      `Iterator[e]^` is exclusive: a method which advances it is an `update def`, a combinator
      which takes it over is a `consume def`, and a second use of a consumed iterator does not
      compile. There is no `hasNext`/`next()` pair. The three honest ways to read one are:

      - a **terminal operation** — `#each`, `#fold`, `#exists`, `#all`, `#count`, `#size`,
        `#seek`, `#chain`, `#to` — which drains the iterator where it is; `each` is the loop;
      - **`#next`**, for a stateful consumer which holds the iterator in a field (a `Stream`
        source, an event pump): one call advances and yields `element | Iterator.Exhausted`,
        and the caller matches on `.Exhausted` before it has an element;
      - the **moving style**, `#pull`, for a hand-written loop which needs the element and the
        right to stop between elements: it consumes the iterator and yields `.Exhausted` or a
        `.Cursor` which owns it; `cursor.value` reads the element and `cursor.pull` consumes
        the cursor to advance. Nothing allocates: the cursor is the iterator reference and the
        sentinel is a `null`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      def total(consume pull: Iterator.Pull[Int], sum: Int): Int = pull match
        case Iterator.Exhausted           => sum
        case cursor: Iterator.Cursor[Int] =>
          val value = cursor.value
          total(cursor.pull, sum + value)

      total(Iterator(1, 2, 3, 4).pull, 0)   // 10
      ```

      Read `value` before `pull` in the same expression, branch with a typed pattern, and
      rebind the handle each step; reusing a pulled cursor, or reading `value` with no cursor,
      is rejected by the compiler.

      The lazy combinators — `#map`, `#filter`, `#sweep`, `#keep`, `#skip`, `#zip`,
      `#indexed`, `#trace`, `#excerpt`, `#batched`, `#deduplicate`, `#distinct`, `#concat` and
      `#flatMap` — consume the iterator they transform and *share* the lambda they are given,
      so the result's capture set names the lambda (`Iterator[e2]^{lambda, caps.any}`): a
      lambda stays usable afterwards, and a lambda which captures an exclusive value makes the
      result exclusive too. A pipeline is grown one consuming stage at a time:

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label A source
        code
            Iterator(1, 2, 3, 4, 5)
      frame
        label Transformed lazily
        code
            Iterator(1, 2, 3, 4, 5).map(_*10)
      frame
        label Filtered, still lazily
        code
            Iterator(1, 2, 3, 4, 5).map(_*10).filter(_ > 10)
      frame
        label Swept by a partial function
        code
            Iterator(1, 2, 3, 4, 5).map(_*10).filter(_ > 10).sweep { case n if n != 40 => n/10 }
      frame
        label Drained into a shape
        code
            Iterator(1, 2, 3, 4, 5).map(_*10).filter(_ > 10).sweep { case n if n != 40 => n/10 }.to[List]   // List(2, 3, 5)
      ##
      ```

      An implementation writes `new Iterator[e]:` with two members and inherits the rest:
      `update def advance(): Boolean`, which moves to the next element and materialises it
      into a `var`, and `def current: e`, a plain unboxed read. Both are gated to this package
      and to implementors, so a caller cannot read `current` without having advanced. A class
      cannot hold a fresh iterator in a `val` ("hides enclosing instance"): mint it in a
      factory and capture the local, re-create it per access over shared state, or take it as
      a constructor parameter.

      An `Iterator` is deliberately *not* `Traversable`: a traversal borrows, and borrowing an
      exclusive iterator into a result which outlives the call is what separation checking
      forbids. So it drains with its own `#to` member rather than the generic `to`, and
      `#flatMap`'s lambda returns a *collection* (anything `Traversable`), never an iterator —
      a lambda cannot hand back a freshly minted capability through a function type.

      The standard library and Java are reached only at a boundary: `.from` wraps a
      `scala.collection.Iterator`, a `java.util.Iterator` or a `java.util.Enumeration` on the
      way in, and `#stdlib` hands one out to an API which insists on it.
  refer murmuration.Traversable
  refer murmuration.Reshapable
  entry .Exhausted   the sentinel for "no more elements", as both a type and the value
      An opaque type `<: Matchable & caps.Pure`, backed by `null` so it costs nothing, and
      the value of that type. Spelt as vacuous spells `Unset` (which this module, below
      vacuous, cannot name): a union `element | Iterator.Exhausted` is pure whenever
      `element` is, and `.nn` does not strip it. Matched first — `case Iterator.Exhausted =>` —
      in a `#next` loop or a `#pull` match; `CanEqual` instances let it be compared with `==`
      against any value.
  entry .exhaustedEquality   `CanEqual[value, Exhausted]`, so `next() == Iterator.Exhausted` typechecks
  entry .equalityExhausted   `CanEqual[Exhausted, value]`, the same comparison the other way round
  entry .Cursor   the linear token over a present element, owning the iterator it came from
    detail
        `Cursor[element]^` is the iterator reference itself, opaque so it cannot be forged and
        bounded by `Stateful` so a `match` can test for it against `.Exhausted` (`case cursor:
        Iterator.Cursor[Int]`; backtick a class type parameter in the pattern). `.value` reads
        the element and `.pull` consumes the cursor to advance, yielding the next `.Pull`.
        Separation checking rejects a cursor used after it was pulled.
    entry .apply   wraps a consumed iterator as a cursor; what `Iterator#pull` returns on success
    entry .value   the element under the cursor, read without advancing
    entry .pull   consumes the cursor and advances: the next cursor, or `Exhausted`
  entry .Pull   `Exhausted | Cursor[element]^`: what `pull` yields
  entry .array   an iterator over a JVM array that no writer reaches while it runs
      `Iterator.array(array)` over a `scala.Array[e]^{caps.any.rd}`, which the frozen array's
      `Traversable` instances use. A primitive element is boxed as the standard library's
      array iterator boxes it.
  entry .empty   an iterator with no elements
  entry .apply   `Iterator(a, b, c)`: an iterator over the given elements
  entry .range   `Iterator.range(start, end)` or `(start, end, step)`: the integers from `start` up to `end`, exclusive
      With a negative `step` the range counts down: `Iterator.range(10, 0, -3)` yields `10, 7,
      4, 1`.
  entry .fill   `Iterator.fill(n)(element)`: `element` evaluated afresh `n` times
  entry .tabulate   `Iterator.tabulate(n)(lambda)`: `lambda(0)` to `lambda(n - 1)`
  entry .continually   `Iterator.continually(element)`: `element` evaluated afresh without end
  entry .iterate   `Iterator.iterate(initial)(lambda)`: `initial`, then `lambda` applied again and again
  entry .from   wraps a stdlib `Iterator`, a `java.util.Iterator` or a `java.util.Enumeration`
      The boundary in from the standard library and Java: the one-shot source's remaining
      elements are taken over by this iterator. The stdlib overload consumes its argument.
  entry #pull   consumes the iterator: a `Cursor` over its first element, or `Exhausted`
      The moving style. A `consume def`, so the iterator is gone after the call and only the
      cursor (if any) remains; `cursor.value` reads, `cursor.pull` advances.
  entry #next   advances and yields the next element, or `Exhausted` once the source is spent
      For a stateful consumer which holds the iterator in a field, where a linear token
      cannot live. There is no `next` without a `hasNext` because they are the same call: match
      `case Iterator.Exhausted => … case value: Int => …`. After exhaustion every call yields
      `Exhausted` again. An opaque element type such as `Text` cannot be type-tested in the
      match; use `#option` or `#seek` there.
  entry #each   visits every remaining element, draining the iterator
  entry #fold   accumulates a state over the remaining elements, draining the iterator
  entry #exists   whether some remaining element satisfies the predicate, stopping there
      Stops at the first match, so the elements after it remain.
  entry #all   whether every remaining element satisfies the predicate, stopping at the first that does not
  entry #count   how many remaining elements satisfy the predicate, draining the iterator
  entry #seek   the first element satisfying a predicate, handed to a continuation; the rest left unconsumed
      `iterator.seek(predicate)(absent)(present)`: two continuations rather than an `Optional`
      result, so the element reaches the caller at its own type with no test and no cast
      (this module cannot name `Optional`; rudiments' `find` is `seek(p)(Unset)(identity(_))`).
      The elements after the match remain: `Iterator(1, 2, 3, 4).seek(_ > 1)(-1)(identity(_))`
      is `2`, leaving `3, 4`.
  entry #option   the next element as a stdlib `Option`, for lazy stdlib shapes that pull through a closure
  entry #chain   the remaining elements as a lazy `Chain`, pulled one cell at a time as it is forced
      The one shape which lets an iterator outlive its owner: the chain memoizes what it has
      pulled, and nothing records the iterator it pulls from, since a `Chain` is pure.
  entry #to   `iterator.to[List]`, `to[Set]`, …: drains into a collection of the given shape
      Through `Reshapable`, with the shape named as a type constructor. A member rather than the
      generic `to` because an iterator is not `Traversable` (a traversal borrows, which an
      exclusive one cannot allow), and a member rather than a companion extension because
      rudiments' lexically-scoped generic `to` would outrank an extension.
  entry #size   how many elements remain, by draining them
  entry #map   each remaining element through the lambda, lazily; consumes the receiver
  entry #filter   the remaining elements satisfying the predicate, lazily; consumes the receiver
  entry #sweep   filter and map in one pass through a partial function, lazily
  entry #flatMap   each element to a *collection*, whose elements are yielded in turn
      The lambda returns anything `Traversable` — a `List`, a `Set`, a `Text` — never an
      iterator, since a lambda cannot hand back a freshly minted capability through a function
      type; the inner traversal is opened here as the result advances.
      `Iterator(1, 2, 3).flatMap(n => List.fill(n)(n)).to[List]` is `List(1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3)`.
  entry #zip   this iterator's elements paired with another's, stopping at the shorter; consumes both
  entry #indexed   each element paired with its position from zero, as an `Int`
      An `Int`, not an `Ordinal`, because denominative sits above this module; rudiments'
      `indexed` on a collection converts to ordinals.
  entry #keep   the first `n` elements, or the longest leading run satisfying a predicate; lazily
      `keep(3)` is the standard library's `take`, `keep(_ < 7)` its `takeWhile`.
  entry #skip   everything after the first `n` elements, or after the leading run satisfying a predicate
      `skip(2)` is the standard library's `drop`, `skip(_ < 2)` its `dropWhile`.
  entry #trace   every intermediate state of a fold, initial first; the stdlib's `scanLeft`
  entry #excerpt   the elements from position `from` (inclusive) to `until` (exclusive)
  entry #deduplicate   the first occurrence of each key, in first-occurrence order
  entry #distinct   the first occurrence of each element, in first-occurrence order
  entry #batched   consecutive batches of at most `n` elements, each a `List`; the last may be shorter
  entry #concat   this iterator's elements, then another's; consumes both
      The standard library's `++`.
  entry #stdlib   a `scala.collection.Iterator` which drains this one; the boundary to the stdlib and Java
      Consumes this iterator: the stdlib view owns it from here on. The view is typed pure, as
      every stdlib iterator is, so it can be handed to `IterableOnce` factories and JDK APIs.
      A tagged bridge, like every `.stdlib`; prefer a native form (`#to`, `#each`, `#chain`)
      wherever one exists.
